    id: "6e1621e9c87dfa9864db",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Germany's far-right AfD says 'democracy demands' parties work with them after state election win",
    summary: "The far-right party is trying to form a government in the state after falling three seats short of a majority.",
    preview: "The far-right Alternative for Germany (AfD) is \"very, very close\" to taking power in the eastern state of Saxony-Anhalt, its deputy parliamentary leader has told the BBC, after a historic victory in elections there. Sunday's preliminary results show it fell three seats short of an outright majority, with 43.8% of the vote. It is now seeking support from other parties or individuals in order to govern.",
    body: "The far-right Alternative for Germany (AfD) is \"very, very close\" to taking power in the eastern state of Saxony-Anhalt, its deputy parliamentary leader has told the BBC, after a historic victory in elections there.\n\nSunday's preliminary results show it fell three seats short of an outright majority, with 43.8% of the vote. It is now seeking support from other parties or individuals in order to govern.\n\nWhile most German political parties refuse to work with the AfD, Beatrix von Storch called for its opponents to enter talks and said: \"You cannot say, I do not accept the vote of the people\".\n\nGermany's Chancellor Friedrich Merz said he was \"deeply shocked\" by the result, describing it as \"seismic... not just in the country but also in my own political party and I take that very seriously\".\n\nHis party, the conservative CDU, saw its share of the vote almost halved to 17.2% in the state's preliminary results.\n\nMerz said it was not possible to return to \"business as usual\", adding that the country needed \"profound reforms\" and the CDU needed to \"reach people at an emotional level\".\n\nNo far-right party has controlled a German state since World War Two, and governing at state level would mean powers over policing and local education.\n\nVon Storch said the state's results \"were on the table and now democrats have to talk to [one] another. This is what democracy demands\".\n\nShe added that if parties refused to work with the AfD, \"then we might end up in severe chaos and maybe we have a new election\".\n\nHer comments were echoed by AfD's leader in Saxony-Anhalt, Ulrich Siegmund, who told a news conference on Monday that the party had won a \"clear mandate to govern\".\n\nThe state's defeated premier, the CDU's Sven Schulze, also said the mandate to govern Saxony-Anhalt \"now lies with the AfD\".\n\nSaxony-Anhalt has a small population of 2.1 million and the AfD in the state is assessed by domestic intelligence as \"right-wing extremist\", a classification rejected by Siegmund, 35.\n\nThe party's plans for Saxony-Anhalt, which include segregating child refugees into special classes and setting up a task-force to help detain and deport migrants who no longer have the right to be in the state, are highly controversial within Germany.\n\nThe AfD is currently heading for 39 seats in the 83-seat state parliament instead of the 42 needed for an outright majority, although the result has not yet been finalised.\n\nVon Storch told the BBC the AfD was talking to individuals within the CDU group - the largest party in both the state's outgoing coalition and Germany's government - because they were \"very, very unhappy with their own party\".\n\nWhat could happen now?\n\nOne idea being floated is an informal deal whereby a small party - the BSW - could give their tacit support to allow the AfD to form a minority government.\n\nThe left-wing populist BSW, which is set to win five seats, is also tough on immigration and backs importing Russian gas.\n\nSiegmund said discussions with other parties were initiated on Sunday night and could potentially take days or weeks to conclude.\n\n\"We reach out to everybody first, we will have consultations and then we'll see whether [there are] individual parliamentary groups or MPs who would be willing to help us,\" he added.\n\nAnother possibility - at least in theory - is a messy, minority coalition government led by the conservative CDU party. But that appears very unlikely.\n\nThere's also the prospect of another election to break the deadlock - an idea which has been mooted by Siegmund who has said he doesn't want to compromise on his policy goals.\n\nThe AfD leads the opinion polls nationally, ahead of the ruling conservatives of Merz and the Social Democrats, but their heartland lies in the eastern states.\n\nThey also won the 2024 state election in neighbouring Thuringia, but failed to secure enough seats to win power.\n\nMerz described the Saxony-Anhalt defeat as the worst the CDU had suffered \"in years, if not decades\", acknowledging that the results in upcoming regional elections in MecklenburgWestern Pomerania and Berlin could have \"increasing significance\".\n\nAmong the AfD's policies is a highly controversial plan for \"remigration\", which is widely understood to mean mass deportations of migrants, although the party says it refers to deporting criminals and those in the country illegally.\n\nCommentators and political opponents called AfD's victory a watershed and break with the past.\n\n\"This isn't just about Saxony-Anhalt,\" Siegmund told supporters overnight. \"It's a signal for the whole of Germany - a self-confident signal.\"\n\nOn Sunday, US President Donald Trump posted a screenshot of the state's projected results on his Truth Social platform. Russian presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev then took a screengrab of Trump's post and praised a \"historic win for the pragmatic AfD\".\n\nDmitriev had earlier praised the Russia-friendly AfD as \"the party of hope and reason, co-operation truth and hope for Germany\". Ahead of Germany's federal election last year, US Vice-President JD Vance endorsed the party, in a move criticised in Germany as political interference.\n\nPolish centre-right Prime Minister Donald Tusk saw the result very differently, saying \"in Poland only idiots and traitors can rejoice at the triumph of the AfD\".\n\nAsked about those who had expressed fears over the far-right party gaining power in the state, deputy parliamentary leader Beatrix von Storch told the BBC that its supporters were afraid of \"losing our economy, they are afraid of losing our inner security\".\n\nShe said conservative voters were turning to alternative political parties because they were not being listened to.\n\nShe added that the party's detractors should: \"Wake up and realise what happened in several other European states... The conservative parties will disappear from the landscape as it happened in Italy, as it's about to happen in the UK, as it might also happen in France\".\n\n- Published19 hours ago\n\n- Published23 hours ago\n\n- Published3 days ago",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "BBC World",
    url: "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cpd0113082eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    time: "14 ore fa",
    pub_ts: 1788789841,
    published_at: "2026-09-07T14:04:01+00:00",
    language: "en",
    score: 2,
