    id: "3f1430928d2e79048b5e",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Ukraine's chief prosecutor resigns over call centre corruption scandal",
    summary: "In his resignation letter, Ruslan Kravchenko denied the allegations against him, saying he did not want his office \"to be used as a tool in political confrontation\".",
    preview: "Ukraine's chief prosecutor Ruslan Kravchenko has resigned over corruption allegations related to illegal call centres in the country. It follows a search of his offices over the weekend by Ukrainian anti-corruption investigators, who said they had identified a money-laundering scheme led by an official in Kravchenko's office. In his resignation letter, Kravchenko said he did not want his position to be used as a \"tool for political confrontation\", and referenced an earlier post in which he rejected the claims as \"unsubstantiated\".",
    body: "Ukraine's chief prosecutor Ruslan Kravchenko has resigned over corruption allegations related to illegal call centres in the country.\n\nIt follows a search of his offices over the weekend by Ukrainian anti-corruption investigators, who said they had identified a money-laundering scheme led by an official in Kravchenko's office.\n\nIn his resignation letter, Kravchenko said he did not want his position to be used as a \"tool for political confrontation\", and referenced an earlier post in which he rejected the claims as \"unsubstantiated\".\n\nCorruption scandals have plagued Kyiv throughout the war against Russia, with accusations levelled at several members of President Volodymyr Zelensky's inner circle.\n\nAccording to Ukraine's National Anti-Corruption Bureau (Nabu) and Specialised Anti-Corruption Prosecutor's Office (Sap), the network in Kravchenko's office received money to protect call centres that carried out phone scams and \"laundered millions in assets\".\n\nThe money was then used to buy property, jewellery, and other valuables, according to Nabu.\n\nKravchenko released a statement on Sunday evening dismissing the allegations as \"completely unsubstantiated\", and denied any wrongdoing.\n\nHe tendered his resignation a day later, thanking Zelensky and the Ukrainian parliament for their \"trust\". The Ukrainian president is yet to comment publicly on the resignation.\n\nDespite the creation of two Ukrainian anti-corruption bodies, Nabu and Sap, over a decade ago, corruption continues to be a significant problem in the country.\n\nIn 2025, nationwide protests broke out after Zelensky tried to implement reforms that would curb the independence of Nabu and Sap.\n\nKyiv's European partners expressed alarm at the announcement, with ambassadors from the G7 group of nations warning it could endanger Ukraine's integration into the European Union.\n\nUkraine is an EU candidate country, a status it was granted on condition it mounted a credible fight against corruption.\n\nZelensky reinstated the freedom of the two anti-corruption bodies as a result of the backlash, but questions have persisted over his commitment to anti-corruption reform.\n\nLast month, Nabu said it had uncovered a scheme to misappropriate more than $3m (£2.2m) in Ukrainian currency to pay bail for a former energy minister accused of money laundering.\n\nA senior official in Zelensky's office alleged to be involved was sacked.\n\nIn an interview with the BBC last month, Ukraine's former defence minister Mykhailo Fedorov said the president needed to be asked about his awareness of potential corruption among his closest team.\n\n\"Let him answer this question... I believe that he's ready for such questions. It's his responsibility to tell us all whether he was aware of all that or not... I don't want to make any assumptions,\" he said.\n\nDuring his time in office, the 35-year-old was credited with heading a drive against corruption.\n\nSince his dismissal in mid-July, after reportedly falling out with the head of Ukraine's armed forces, he has been outspoken against what he has called a \"systemic crisis of governance\" in Ukraine.\n\nHowever, he has emphasised that he has no evidence that Zelensky is personally involved in corruption.\n\n\"In all the time that I worked with him, not a single time have I received from him any task or assignment that would go against law or my sense of integrity,\" Federov told the BBC.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Published12 November 2025",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "BBC World",
    url: "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c780nlgyd79o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    time: "2 ore fa",
    pub_ts: 1788829667,
    published_at: "2026-09-08T01:07:47+00:00",
